{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2131201979/17/des.md\"","aggregated_output":"<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：根据围栏颜色在色板中查找对应的填充纹理模板，将该模板平铺填满围栏围住的封闭区域。\n- `Input structure`：背景为占主导地位的单一颜色。网格中散布着若干由不同颜色像素围成的封闭矩形围栏（围栏宽度为一个像素，围栏内部全是背景色）。网格的一条边缘处有一个色板区域，色板由若干小格子组成，每个小格子上半部分是一个围栏颜色的单像素标记，下半部分是一个 2×2 或 3×3 的小纹理图案（由两种颜色交替排列构成，如棋盘格、条纹等）。每种围栏颜色在色板中恰好出现一次。\n- `Rule`：\n  - 找到网格边缘的色板区域，读取每个围栏颜色与其对应纹理图案的映射关系。\n  - 对网格中每个封闭矩形围栏，确定该围栏的颜色，在色板中查到对应纹理图案。\n  - 将该纹理图案以平铺方式（从围栏内部左上角开始，按行按列周期性重复）填满围栏内部的所有背景像素。\n  - 围栏本身保持不变；色板区域保持不变。\n- `Key insight`：围栏颜色不是直接决定填充色，而是作为索引去色板中查找一个二维纹理模板；解题者需要先识别色板的结构（颜色标记→纹理图案的映射），再将纹理平铺到对应围栏的内部区域。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：将网格中散落的碎片按颜色配对，每对碎片沿其连线中点做镜像对称合并，形成一个完整的对称图形。\n- `Input structure`：背景为单一主色。网格中有若干前景色碎片，每种非背景颜色恰好出现两个分离的 4-连通对象。同一颜色的两个碎片形状互为某种镜像关系（水平、垂直或对角镜像），但被分别放置在网格的不同位置。两个碎片的像素总数相同。\n- `Rule`：\n  - 对每种前景颜色，找到其两个分离的 4-连通对象。\n  - 计算两个对象各自包围盒中心的中点位置，该中点即为合并后图形的中心。\n  - 将两个碎片同时平移，使它们各自的包围盒中心到达关于该中点对称的位置。平移后两个碎片叠加在一起，形成一个关于中点对称的完整图形。\n  - 若平移后某些像素重叠，保留该颜色即可（同色重叠无影响）。\n  - 输出网格尺寸不变，所有背景像素保持不变。\n- `Key insight`：两个同色碎片不是简单拼接，而是需要找到它们的\"虚拟对称中心\"（两个包围盒中心的中点），然后将碎片向该中心靠拢，直到形成完美对称图形。解题者需要发现同色碎片互为镜像这一隐含关系。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：网格中每个封闭房间内的箭头指示了该房间内容应被复制到的相邻房间，所有房间同时执行复制操作后得到输出。\n- `Input structure`：背景为黑色。网格被灰色的单像素宽直线分割成若干大小相同的矩形房间（类似井字格或更大的网格）。每个房间内部要么是空的（全为黑色背景），要么包含一个由某种非黑非灰颜色绘制的小图案。此外，部分房间在其边缘处有一个由 red 颜色绘制的箭头标记：箭头由三个像素组成，一个像素在房间的边缘中点位置，另外两个像素在其相邻位置形成指向上、下、左或右的箭头形状（即一个像素杆加两个像素的箭头尖）。箭头方向指向相邻房间。\n- `Rule`：\n  - 识别灰色分隔线，确定房间网格的行列结构和每个房间的边界。\n  - 对每个含有图案和箭头的房间，读取箭头方向，确定目标房间（箭头所指方向的相邻房间）。\n  - 将该房间内的图案（不包括箭头本身）复制到目标房间中，保持图案在房间内的相对位置不变。\n  - 源房间的图案在复制后被清除（变为黑色背景），箭头标记也被清除。\n  - 若目标房间原本就有图案，则两个图案叠加（像素取非背景色的那个；若两者都非背景色且不同色，取被复制过来的颜色覆盖）。\n  - 无箭头的房间保持原样（除非被其他房间的箭头指定为目标）。\n- `Key insight`：解题者需要先识别网格的房间结构，再识别箭头的方向语义（箭头是搬运指令而非装饰），最后理解所有搬运同时发生——源房间清空、目标房间接收。多个房间可能指向同一目标房间，导致图案叠加。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：每个前景对象根据其内部孔洞的数量，在输出中被替换为对应层数的同心边框环。\n- `Input structure`：背景为单一主色（如黑色）。网格中有若干分离的前景对象，每个对象由单一非背景颜色构成，形状各异（可能是实心的，也可能包含一个或多个被该颜色完全包围的背景色孔洞）。每个对象的包围盒互不重叠。每个对象的孔洞数量在 0 到 4 之间。\n- `Rule`：\n  - 对每个前景对象，计算其内部封闭孔洞的数量 N（孔洞定义为被该对象颜色完全围住的、不与网格边界相连的背景色连通区域）。\n  - 在该对象的包围盒区域内，用同色绘制 N+1 层同心矩形边框环（最外层边框与包围盒边缘重合，每层向内缩进一个像素，环之间留一个像素的背景色间隔）。\n  - 若包围盒太小无法容纳 N+1 层环，则尽可能绘制，从外向内绘制直到空间用尽。\n  - 不在任何对象包围盒内的背景像素保持不变。\n- `Key insight`：对象的原始形状被完全丢弃，仅保留两个信息：颜色和孔洞数量。孔洞数量决定了输出中同心环的层数。解题者需要发现\"孔洞计数→环层数\"这一非直觉映射关系，而不是试图从对象的外部轮廓推断输出形状。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：网格中有两个独立区块：一个是由彩色像素构成的\"指令序列\"，另一个是待操作的目标图案；指令序列中每个颜色代表一种空间变换，按从左到右的顺序依次作用于目标图案。\n- `Input structure`：背景为黑色。网格被明确分为上下两个区域（由一条横跨全宽的灰色水平线分隔）。上方区域包含一行单像素高的彩色像素序列（从左到右排列，每个像素颜色不同或相同，颜色来自固定的四种：red 表示顺时针旋转 90°，blue 表示水平翻转，green 表示垂直翻转，yellow 表示转置即沿主对角线翻转）。下方区域包含一个由若干非背景颜色构成的小型方阵图案（边长不超过 5），图案位于下方区域的左上角。\n- `Rule`：\n  - 读取上方的指令序列，从左到右依次获得每一步的变换操作。\n  - 从下方区域提取初始图案。\n  - 按指令序列的顺序，依次对图案施加对应的空间变换（旋转、翻转或转置），每步变换作用于上一步的结果。\n  - 将最终变换后的图案放回下方区域的左上角位置，清除下方区域其他像素。\n  - 上方指令区域在输出中被完全清除（变为黑色）。\n- `Key insight`：解题者需要首先破解颜色与变换类型的对应关系（这需要跨多个样例比对才能确定），然后理解变换是按顺序链式施加的而非独立施加。多个样例中指令序列长度和颜色组合不同，解题者必须从最终结果反推每种颜色代表的操作。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：识别网格中每条\"河流\"（连续背景色通道）穿过的所有\"桥梁\"（横跨河流的前景色短线段），并将桥梁颜色替换为该河流两岸出现次数最多的前景颜色。\n- `Input structure`：背景为单一主色。网格中有若干宽度为一个像素的水平或垂直背景色通道（\"河流\"），这些通道横贯网格的一整行或一整列。河流将网格分隔成若干非背景色的区块（\"陆地\"）。在河流上方，偶尔有长度为一个像素、颜色为特殊标记色（如灰色）的短线段横跨河流连接两侧陆地（\"桥梁\"），桥梁方向垂直于河流方向。每块陆地内部由一种主要前景颜色填充（可能混有少量其他颜色像素）。\n- `Rule`：\n  - 找到所有河流（横贯整行或整列的背景色通道）。\n  - 找到河流上的所有桥梁（垂直于河流方向的灰色单像素线段）。\n  - 对每个桥梁，观察其两端连接的两块陆地，确定这两块陆地中占主导地位的前景颜色（即两块陆地中像素数量最多的那种非背景色）。\n  - 将桥梁的灰色替换为该主导前景颜色。\n  - 其余所有像素保持不变。\n- `Key insight`：桥梁的最终颜色不是由桥梁自身决定，而是由它所连接的两岸\"陆地\"的主导颜色共同投票决定。解题者需要先理解河流-桥梁-陆地的拓扑结构，再理解颜色传播的投票机制。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：在网格中找到所有相同形状的对象组，每组中保留面积最大的一个实例，其余缩放为与最大实例相同大小后叠放到最大实例的位置上，颜色按层级混合。\n- `Input structure`：背景为单一主色（如黑色）。网格中有若干前景对象，每个对象是一个单色的连通区域。不同对象可能有不同颜色和不同大小，但某些对象的形状在归一化（缩放到相同大小）后是完全相同的。形状相同的对象构成一组，每组至少有两个对象。每组中对象的大小各不相同（即同组内没有两个对象面积相同）。\n- `Rule`：\n  - 将所有前景对象按归一化形状分组（忽略大小和颜色，仅看形状轮廓是否经缩放后一致）。\n  - 对每组，找到面积最大的对象作为\"锚点\"，记录其位置和大小。\n  - 将同组内其他对象从原位置清除，将它们的颜色按面积从大到小的顺序依次覆盖到锚点对象的形状上：最大的对象颜色在最底层，第二大的对象颜色覆盖锚点形状内部缩小一圈的区域，以此类推，每层向内缩进一个像素。\n  - 不属于任何组的孤立对象（形状唯一）保持不变。\n- `Key insight`：解题者需要发现不同大小的对象实际上是同一形状的缩放版本，然后理解它们按大小层叠合并到最大实例处，每个尺寸的颜色形成一层同心嵌套结构。核心在于\"形状归一化匹配\"这一非显然的分组标准。\n</new_puzzle>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfiles = [\n'/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/8b7bacbf.py',\n'/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/9841fdad.py',\n'/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/dbff022c.py',\n'/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/88e364bc.py',\n'/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/247ef758.py',\n'/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/332f06d7.py',\n'/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/aa62e3f4.py',\n'/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/5adee1b2.py',\n]\nfor path in files:\n    text = Path(path).read_text()\n    print(f'===== {Path(path).name} =====')\n    for name in ['def main', 'def generate_input']:\n        idx = text.find(name)\n        if idx == -1:\n            continue\n        start = text.rfind('\\\\n', 0, idx)\n        snippet = text[idx:text.find('\\\\n\\\\ndef ', idx+1) if text.find('\\\\n\\\\ndef ', idx+1) \"'!= -1 else len(text)]\n        lines = snippet.splitlines()\n        print('\"'\\\\n'.join(lines[:80]))\n        print('...')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfiles = [\n'/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/8b7bacbf.py',\n'/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/9841fdad.py',\n'/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/dbff022c.py',\n'/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/88e364bc.py',\n'/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/247ef758.py',\n'/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/332f06d7.py',\n'/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/aa62e3f4.py',\n'/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/5adee1b2.py',\n]\nfor path in files:\n    text = Path(path).read_text()\n    print(f'===== {Path(path).name} =====')\n    for name in ['def main', 'def generate_input']:\n        idx = text.find(name)\n        if idx == -1:\n            continue\n        start = text.rfind('\\\\n', 0, idx)\n        snippet = text[idx:text.find('\\\\n\\\\ndef ', idx+1) if text.find('\\\\n\\\\ndef ', idx+1) \"'!= -1 else len(text)]\n        lines = snippet.splitlines()\n        print('\"'\\\\n'.join(lines[:80]))\n        print('...')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"===== 8b7bacbf.py =====\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Fill enclosed regions along a line seeded by unique border pixels.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - bg = most common color.\n    - Colored lines/drawings on the grid.\n    - Unique single-pixel colors on the grid border mark seed endpoints of each line.\n\n    Rule:\n    - Each border seed pixel connects (8-connected) to a line drawing.\n    - Find closed bg cavities whose boundary touches that line.\n    - Flood-fill each such cavity with the seed pixel's color.\n\n    Key insight: Only cavities reachable through the seed's own line get filled; the seed's unique color acts as a per-line fill label.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    h, w = g.shape\n    bg = get_bg(g)\n\n    out = g.copy()\n\n    # Precompute background regions (4-connected). Exclude those containing background corners as \"outside\".\n    bg_comps = _components_mask(g == bg, conn8=False)\n    regions = []  # list of (set_pts, boundary_set, touches_border, size)\n    for pts in bg_comps:\n        region = set(pts)\n        touches = _touches_border(pts, h, w)\n        boundary = set()\n        for r, c in region:\n            for nr, nc in neighbors4(r, c):\n                if 0 <= nr < h and 0 <= nc < w and (nr, nc) not in region and int(g[nr, nc]) != bg:\n                    boundary.add((nr, nc))\n        regions.append((region, boundary, touches, len(region)))\n\n    corners = {(0, 0), (0, w - 1), (h - 1, 0), (h - 1, w - 1)}\n    outside_idxs = set()\n    for i, (reg, _, _, _) in enumerate(regions):\n        if any(p in reg for p in corners if int(g[p]) == bg):\n            outside_idxs.add(i)\n\n    cavities = []\n    for i, (reg, bnd, _, _) in enumerate(regions):\n        if i in outside_idxs or not bnd:\n            continue\n        bcols = {int(g[p]) for p in bnd}\n        cavities.append((reg, bnd, bcols))\n\n    for sr, sc in _seed_pixels(g, bg):\n        seed_col = int(g[sr, sc])\n\n        neigh_cols = []\n        for nr, nc in neighbors4(sr, sc):\n            if 0 <= nr < h and 0 <= nc < w:\n                v = int(g[nr, nc])\n                if v != bg:\n                    neigh_cols.append(v)\n        assert neigh_cols, \"seed pixel must have at least one non-bg neighbor\"\n        line_col = Counter(neigh_cols).most_common(1)[0][0]\n\n        # Trace the connected \"drawing\" reachable from the seed (8-connected, non-background).\n        traced = _nonbg_component8_from(g, (sr, sc), bg)\n\n        for cav, boundary, bcols in cavities:\n            if len(bcols) != 1:\n                continue\n            boundary_in_traced = boundary & traced\n            if not boundary_in_traced:\n                continue\n\n            # Require that the traced boundary is adjacent (8-neigh) to at least one pixel of the line color.\n            # This prevents filling regions that are only connected through other structures, not the line itself.\n            ok = False\n            for r, c in boundary_in_traced:\n                for nr, nc in neighbors8(r, c):\n                    if 0 <= nr < h and 0 <= nc < w and int(g[nr, nc]) == line_col:\n                        ok = True\n                        break\n                if ok:\n                    break\n...\ndef generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n    rng = random.Random()\n    for _ in range(GEN_RETRIES):\n        g = _gen_attempt(rng)\n        if g is not None:\n            return g\n    raise RuntimeError(\"generate_input: exhausted attempts\")\n...\n===== 9841fdad.py =====\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Expand the colored runs from the left reference panel into the blank right panel.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - A uniform rectangular frame surrounds the grid.\n    - One full-height divider of the frame color splits a left reference panel\n      from a blank right panel.\n    - Both panels have one background margin column on each side of their active\n      columns; the left active columns contain two-column endpoint slots, possibly\n      connected by same-color horizontal runs.\n\n    Rule:\n    - Keep the original grid and paint only in the right panel's active columns.\n    - A source run exactly matching the left endpoint slot is copied to the left\n      two columns of the right active span.\n    - A source run exactly matching the right endpoint slot is copied to the right\n      two columns of the right active span.\n    - A source run connecting the endpoint slots is stretched across the entire\n      right active span.\n\n    Key insight:\n    The left panel is a compressed legend for the right panel: endpoint markers\n    stay two pixels wide, while connectors between endpoints expand to fill the\n    distance between them.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    assert g.ndim == 2, \"expected a 2D grid\"\n    H, W = g.shape\n    assert H >= 3 and W >= 7, \"grid too small for framed two-panel structure\"\n\n    frame = int(g[0, 0])\n    assert np.all(g[0, :] == frame) and np.all(g[-1, :] == frame), \"bad horizontal frame\"\n    assert np.all(g[:, 0] == frame) and np.all(g[:, -1] == frame), \"bad vertical frame\"\n\n    divider = _single_divider_column(g, frame)\n    assert divider >= 5 and W - divider >= 6, \"panels must contain margins and active slots\"\n\n    left_panel = g[1 : H - 1, 1:divider]\n    left_bg = get_bg(left_panel)\n    assert np.all(left_panel[:, 0] == left_bg), \"left panel must have a left margin\"\n    assert np.all(left_panel[:, -1] == left_bg), \"left panel must have a right margin\"\n\n    mini_start = 2\n    mini_end = divider - 2\n    assert mini_end - mini_start + 1 >= 4, \"left active region must contain two endpoint slots\"\n    left_slot = (mini_start, mini_start + 1)\n    right_slot = (mini_end - 1, mini_end)\n\n    target_start = divider + 2\n    target_end = W - 3\n    assert target_end - target_start + 1 >= 4, \"right active region must contain endpoint slots\"\n    right_bg = int(g[1, divider + 1])\n    right_panel = g[1 : H - 1, divider + 1 : W - 1]\n    assert np.all(right_panel == right_bg), \"right panel must be blank before expansion\"\n\n    out = g.copy()\n    painted_any = False\n    for r in range(1, H - 1):\n        c = mini_start\n        while c <= mini_end:\n            color = int(g[r, c])\n            if color == left_bg:\n                c += 1\n                continue\n\n            c0 = c\n            while c <= mini_end and int(g[r, c]) == color:\n                c += 1\n            c1 = c - 1\n\n            in_left_slot = (c0, c1) == left_slot\n            in_right_slot = (c0, c1) == right_slot\n            if in_left_slot:\n                d0, d1 = target_start, target_start + 1\n            elif in_right_slot:\n                d0, d1 = target_end - 1, target_end\n            else:\n                assert c0 == left_slot[0] and c1 >= right_slot[0], \"run must connect endpoint slots\"\n                d0, d1 = target_start, target_end\n...\ndef generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n    for _ in range(GENERATION_RETRIES):\n        grid = _gen_attempt()\n        if grid is not None:\n            return grid\n    raise RuntimeError(\"failed to generate a valid 9841fdad input\")\n...\n===== dbff022c.py =====\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Fill hollow sprites' interiors using a color-palette legend on the grid edge.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - bg = most common color. Several hollow sprites with distinct border colors.\n    - A small 2×N or N×2 palette block sits on one grid edge; one row/column lists border colors, the other lists corresponding fill colors.\n\n    Rule:\n    - Locate the palette legend on a grid edge and read it as a border→fill color mapping.\n    - Find enclosed bg regions (holes) inside sprites that don't touch the grid boundary.\n    - For each hole, identify the most common adjacent border color, look it up in the palette, and fill the hole with the mapped fill color.\n\n    Key insight: The palette's edge position and orientation determine reading direction; border colors in the palette uniquely key into fill colors.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    bg = _bg_boundary(g)\n\n    mapping, pal_mask = _palette_mapping(g, bg)\n    assert mapping, \"main: palette mapping must be non-empty\"\n\n    out = g.copy()\n    _fill_enclosed_bg_regions(out, bg, mapping, pal_mask)\n\n    return out\n\n# --------------- generator (c) ---------------\n\n# -- Configuration constants --------------------------------------------------\n# Grid dimensions\n_GRID_H_MIN, _GRID_H_MAX = 4, 13\n_GRID_W_MIN, _GRID_W_MAX = 10, 15\n_SMALL_H_THRESHOLD = 6        # below this, prefer left/right palette\n_SMALL_H_PALETTE_FLIP = 0.7   # probability of flipping top/bottom -> left/right\n_TINY_H_THRESHOLD = 5         # below this, cap sprite height at 3\n\n# Palette\n_PALETTE_N_MIN, _PALETTE_N_MAX = 2, 5\n\n# Sprite geometry\n_SPRITE_H_MAX = 7\n_SPRITE_W_MAX = 6\n_SPRITE_DIM_SUM_CAP = 10      # if sh + sw exceeds this, clamp sw\n_SPRITE_CELL_MAX = 16\n_FILLED_SPRITE_MAX = 3\n\n# Sprite mutation probabilities\n_P_HORIZ_WALL = 0.3           # add horizontal internal wall\n_P_VERT_WALL = 0.3            # add vertical internal wall\n_P_BORDER_REMOVAL = 0.4       # remove border cells for irregular shapes\n_P_DIAMOND = 0.2              # use diamond shape for 3x3\n\n# Generation\n_MAX_RETRIES = 300\n# -- End configuration --------------------------------------------------------\n...\ndef generate_input():\n    for _ in range(_MAX_RETRIES):\n        r = _gen_attempt()\n        if r is not None:\n            return r\n    raise RuntimeError(f\"Failed to generate input after {_MAX_RETRIES} attempts\")\n...\n===== 88e364bc.py =====\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Slide yellow pixels inside walled regions in the direction dictated by border-legend arrows.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - bg = most common color; yellow pixels sit inside enclosed regions bounded by colored walls.\n    - Border legends: small rectangles on the grid edge whose fill color C matches a region's border color. Each legend contains a center dot and an indicator dot encoding a direction.\n\n    Rule:\n    - For each enclosed region, identify its border color C and look up the legend with fill color C.\n    - The direction from the legend's center dot to its indicator dot gives the slide direction.\n    - All yellow pixels in that region slide in that direction, stacking against the far wall.\n\n    Key insight: Legend fill color links to region border color; the center→indicator offset encodes the movement vector.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    h, w = g.shape\n    bg = get_bg(g)\n\n    mover = 4  # yellow in ARC\n    dirs = _legend_directions(g, bg)\n    assert dirs, \"legend_directions must find at least one legend; grid should always have border legends\"\n\n    passable = (g == bg) | (g == mover)\n    out = g.copy()\n\n    # Find enclosed passable regions (not connected to the outer border).\n    for pts in components_mask(passable, connectivity=4):\n        if any(r == 0 or c == 0 or r == h - 1 or c == w - 1 for r, c in pts):\n            continue  # outside background\n\n        movers = [(int(r), int(c)) for r, c in pts if int(g[r, c]) == mover]\n        if not movers:\n            continue\n\n        # Determine this region's border color by counting adjacent wall colors.\n        border_counts: dict[int, int] = {}\n        pset = {(int(r), int(c)) for r, c in pts}\n        for r, c in pts:\n            for nr, nc in neighbors4(r, c):\n                if not (0 <= nr < h and 0 <= nc < w):\n                    continue\n                if (nr, nc) in pset:\n                    continue\n                v = int(g[nr, nc])\n                if v == bg or v == mover:\n                    continue\n                border_counts[v] = border_counts.get(v, 0) + 1\n        if not border_counts:\n            continue\n        border_col = max(border_counts, key=border_counts.get)\n        if border_col not in dirs:\n            continue\n        dr, dc = dirs[border_col]\n\n        # Clear old movers.\n        for r, c in movers:\n            out[r, c] = bg\n\n        # Slide and repaint.\n        new_pts = _slide_points(pset, movers, dr, dc)\n        for r, c in new_pts:\n            out[r, c] = mover\n\n    return out\n\n# --------------- generator (claude-att1) ---------------\n\n\"\"\"Generator for task 88e364bc (slide yellow in walled regions by legend arrows).\"\"\"\nimport random\nimport numpy as np\n\nfrom arc_utils import label_mask, neighbors4, paste\n\n# ── Configuration ─────────────────────────────────────────────────\nGRID_H, GRID_W = 20, 20\n\n# Legend\nCARDINAL_DIRS = [(0, 1), (0, -1), (1, 0), (-1, 0)]\nDIAGONAL_DIRS = [(1, 1), (1, -1), (-1, 1), (-1, -1)]\n...\ndef generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n    rng = random.Random()\n    for _ in range(GEN_RETRIES):\n        try:\n            g = _gen_attempt(rng)\n        except Exception:\n            continue\n        if g is not None:\n            return g\n    raise RuntimeError(\"generate_input: exceeded retries\")\n...\n===== 247ef758.py =====\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Move colored sprites from the left strip into a framed area at marker-defined targets.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - bg background; rectangular frame on right with colored markers on its edges; sprites in a narrow left strip separated by a constant-color divider column.\n\n    Rule:\n    - For each color, target rows/cols are where both opposite frame edges share that color. Targets are the Cartesian product.\n    - Erase each sprite from the left and stamp it centered at every target. Higher sprites cover lower ones.\n\n    Key insight: One sprite replicates to a grid of row × column marker intersections.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    bg = int(get_bg(g))\n\n    # --- frame interior: largest bg component not touching boundary ---\n    inner_bbox = largest_enclosed_component_bbox(g == int(bg), connectivity=4)\n    assert not (inner_bbox is None)\n    ir0, ic0, ir1, ic1 = map(int, inner_bbox)\n    fr0, fc0, fr1, fc1 = int(ir0 - 1), int(ic0 - 1), int(ir1 + 1), int(ic1 + 1)\n\n    # --- divider columns (fixed) on the left side ---\n    divider_mask = np.zeros((H, W), dtype=bool)\n    for c in range(0, max(0, int(fc0))):\n        colv = g[:, int(c)]\n        non = colv[colv != int(bg)]\n        if int(len(non)) >= int(H - 1) and len(set(map(int, non))) == 1:\n            divider_mask[:, int(c)] = True\n\n    left_cols = (np.arange(W)[None, :] < int(fc0))\n\n    # --- sprites: per-color 8-connected components in left area (excluding divider) ---\n    sprites = []\n    for col in sorted(set(map(int, g.flatten())) - {int(bg)}):\n        mask = (g == int(col)) & left_cols & (~divider_mask)\n        if not bool(mask.any()):\n            continue\n        slab, sn = label_mask(mask, connectivity=8)\n        for sid in range(1, int(sn) + 1):\n            pts = np.argwhere(slab == int(sid))\n            r0, c0 = pts.min(axis=0)\n            r1, c1 = pts.max(axis=0)\n            r0, c0, r1, c1 = map(int, (r0, c0, r1, c1))\n            m = slab[int(r0) : int(r1) + 1, int(c0) : int(c1) + 1] == int(sid)\n            sprites.append({\"color\": int(col), \"r0\": int(r0), \"c0\": int(c0), \"r1\": int(r1), \"c1\": int(c1), \"mask\": m})\n\n    move_colors = {int(s[\"color\"]) for s in sprites}\n\n    # --- marker-derived targets (restricted to colors that actually appear as sprites) ---\n    row_marks: dict[int, list[int]] = defaultdict(list)\n    col_marks: dict[int, list[int]] = defaultdict(list)\n    for r in range(int(ir0), int(ir1) + 1):\n        cl = int(g[int(r), int(fc0)])\n        cr = int(g[int(r), int(fc1)])\n        if cl == cr and cl != int(bg) and cl in move_colors:\n            row_marks[int(cl)].append(int(r))\n    for c in range(int(ic0), int(ic1) + 1):\n        ct = int(g[int(fr0), int(c)])\n        cb = int(g[int(fr1), int(c)])\n        if ct == cb and ct != int(bg) and ct in move_colors:\n            col_marks[int(ct)].append(int(c))\n\n    targets: dict[int, list[tuple[int, int]]] = {}\n    for col in set(row_marks.keys()) & set(col_marks.keys()):\n        pts = {(int(r), int(c)) for r in row_marks[int(col)] for c in col_marks[int(col)]}\n        targets[int(col)] = sorted(pts)\n\n    out = g.copy()\n    movers = [s for s in sprites if int(s[\"color\"]) in targets]\n\n    # Erase originals of moved sprites.\n    for s in movers:\n        r0, c0 = int(s[\"r0\"]), int(s[\"c0\"])\n        for rr, cc in np.argwhere(s[\"mask\"]):\n            out[int(r0) + int(rr), int(c0) + int(cc)] = int(bg)\n\n    # Bottom-to-top ordering: lower sprites first, upper sprites last (covering).\n    movers.sort(key=lambda s: (-((int(s[\"r0\"]) + int(s[\"r1\"])) / 2.0), int(s[\"c0\"])))\n...\ndef generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n    for _ in range(300):\n        result = _gen_attempt()\n        if result is not None:\n            return result\n    raise RuntimeError(\"generate_input: failed after 300 attempts\")\n...\n===== 332f06d7.py =====\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Slide a solid square through a winding channel to reach a same-sized target square.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - bg (most common color). A river color forms a winding corridor.\n    - Two same-sized NxN solid squares on the river: mover and destination, each a distinct color.\n\n    Rule:\n    - The mover square travels along the river toward the destination.\n    - It can only pass through positions where the full NxN area is river.\n    - If reachable, the mover replaces the destination; otherwise it stops at the farthest reachable point.\n    - The mover's original position is filled with the river color.\n\n    Key insight: Chokepoints narrower than N pixels block the square, forcing it to stop short.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\n    H, W = g.shape\n\n    # Corner-majority background (more stable than global mode here).\n    bg = int(Counter([int(g[0, 0]), int(g[0, -1]), int(g[-1, 0]), int(g[-1, -1])]).most_common(1)[0][0])\n\n    # Detect solid square components (excluding bg).\n    squares: list[dict] = []\n    for comp in find_components(g, bg=bg, connectivity=4, monochromatic=True, return_format=\"dict\"):\n        col = int(comp[\"color\"])\n        b = comp[\"bbox\"]\n        if bbox_h(b) != bbox_w(b):\n            continue\n        patch = g[int(b[0]) : int(b[2]) + 1, int(b[1]) : int(b[3]) + 1]\n        if not bool((patch == int(col)).all()):\n            continue\n        squares.append({\"col\": int(col), \"r0\": int(b[0]), \"c0\": int(b[1]), \"n\": int(bbox_h(b))})\n\n    assert not (len(squares) < 2)\n\n    # Choose the size that appears at least twice (most common size).\n    by_n: dict[int, list[dict]] = defaultdict(list)\n    for s in squares:\n        by_n[int(s[\"n\"])].append(s)\n    nside = max(by_n.items(), key=lambda kv: (len(kv[1]), kv[0]))[0]\n    cand = by_n[int(nside)]\n    assert not (len(cand) < 2)\n\n    cand.sort(key=lambda s: int(s[\"col\"]))\n    mover = cand[0]\n    dest = cand[1]\n\n    n = int(mover[\"n\"])\n    sr, sc = int(mover[\"r0\"]), int(mover[\"c0\"])\n    dr, dc = int(dest[\"r0\"]), int(dest[\"c0\"])\n\n    # River = most common remaining color (excluding bg and the two square colors).\n    counts = Counter(map(int, g.flatten()))\n    river = None\n    for col, _ct in counts.most_common():\n        if int(col) == int(bg):\n            continue\n        if int(col) in (int(mover[\"col\"]), int(dest[\"col\"])):\n            continue\n        river = int(col)\n        break\n    assert not (river is None)\n\n    trav = (g == int(river)) | (g == int(mover[\"col\"])) | (g == int(dest[\"col\"]))\n\n    # Feasible top-left placements: NxN window fully inside trav.\n    h2 = int(H - n + 1)\n    w2 = int(W - n + 1)\n    assert not (h2 <= 0 or w2 <= 0)\n    assert not (not (0 <= sr < h2 and 0 <= sc < w2))\n\n    feasible = np.zeros((h2, w2), dtype=bool)\n    for r in range(h2):\n        for c in range(w2):\n            if bool(trav[int(r) : int(r + n), int(c) : int(c + n)].all()):\n                feasible[int(r), int(c)] = True\n    assert not (not bool(feasible[int(sr), int(sc)]))\n\n    # BFS on placement grid.\n...\ndef generate_input():\n    for _ in range(300):\n        result = _gen_attempt()\n        if result is not None:\n            return result\n    raise RuntimeError(\"Failed to generate valid input after 300 attempts\")\n\n\n...\n===== aa62e3f4.py =====\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Replace the outlined object with the outside contour of that outline in the marker color.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - A dominant background surrounds one 8-connected foreground outline.\n    - The outline is the unique largest foreground color.\n    - One or more smaller interior colors are inside the outline; the marker color\n      is the unique smallest non-outline color.\n\n    Rule:\n    - Erase all original foreground pixels to background.\n    - Paint every background pixel that is 4-adjacent to the outline with the\n      marker color.\n    - Keep the grid shape unchanged.\n\n    Key insight: The colored interior marker supplies the paint color, while the\n    largest outline supplies only the contour whose outside edge is traced.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    bg = int(get_bg(g))\n\n    counts = Counter(map(int, g.flatten()))\n    foreground_colors = [color for color in counts if color != bg]\n    assert len(foreground_colors) >= 2, \"expected an outline color and a marker color\"\n\n    frame_count = max(counts[color] for color in foreground_colors)\n    frame_color = _unique_color_with_count(counts, foreground_colors, frame_count)\n    assert len(components_mask(g == frame_color, connectivity=8)) == 1, \"outline must be 8-connected\"\n\n    marker_candidates = [color for color in foreground_colors if color != frame_color]\n    marker_count = min(counts[color] for color in marker_candidates)\n    marker_color = _unique_color_with_count(counts, marker_candidates, marker_count)\n\n    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\n    contour: set[tuple[int, int]] = set()\n    for r, c in np.argwhere(g == frame_color):\n        for nr, nc in neighbors4(int(r), int(c)):\n            if 0 <= nr < g.shape[0] and 0 <= nc < g.shape[1] and g[nr, nc] == bg:\n                contour.add((int(nr), int(nc)))\n    assert contour, \"expected outline to touch background contour cells\"\n\n    for r, c in contour:\n        out[r, c] = marker_color\n    return out\n\n# --------------- generator (att2) ---------------\n\nGRID_MIN, GRID_MAX = 5, 16\nN_COLORS = 10\n\nSHAPE_MIN_HEIGHT, SHAPE_MAX_HEIGHT = 5, 7\nSHAPE_MIN_WIDTH, SHAPE_MAX_WIDTH = 3, 5\nSTART_WIDTH_MIN, START_WIDTH_MAX = 1, 2\nMAX_LOCAL_WIDTH = 5\n\nMARKER_MIN_CELLS, MARKER_MAX_CELLS = 1, 3\n\nGEN_ATTEMPTS = 300\n\nEDGE_TOUCH_TOP_BOTTOM = \"TB\"\nCORNER_EDGE_PAIRS = [\"TL\", \"TR\", \"BL\", \"BR\"]\nZERO_DELTA_SHIFTS = [-1, 0, 0, 0, 1]\n...\ndef generate_input():\n    for _ in range(GEN_ATTEMPTS):\n        grid = _gen_attempt()\n        if grid is not None:\n            return grid\n    raise RuntimeError(\"failed to generate a valid aa62e3f4 input\")\n...\n===== 5adee1b2.py =====\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Use the left-edge color legend to draw a padded rectangular halo around each matching object while preserving enclosed holes.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - The grid has a dominant background color.\n    - One or more two-row markers sit on the left edge; each marker is a 2x2 block whose left column is an object color and right column is its halo color.\n    - Away from the markers, every source color from the legend appears as one or more 4-connected monochrome objects.\n\n    Rule:\n    - Keep the input pixels unchanged.\n    - For each source-color object, take its tight bbox expanded by one cell and clipped to the grid.\n    - Recolor background pixels in that expanded region to the marker's halo color, except for background holes enclosed by the object.\n\n    Key insight:\n    - The small left-edge bars are a legend: they map object colors to the colors used for the surrounding padded bbox.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    bg = int(get_bg(g))\n    out = g.copy()\n    h, w = g.shape\n\n    markers: dict[int, int] = {}\n    marker_source_cells: dict[int, set[tuple[int, int]]] = {}\n    for comp in find_components(g, bg=bg, connectivity=4, monochromatic=False, return_format=\"dict\"):\n        r0, c0, r1, c1 = comp[\"bbox\"]\n        height = int(r1 - r0 + 1)\n        if c0 != 0 or c1 != 1 or height != 2 or int(comp[\"area\"]) != 4:\n            continue\n\n        sub = g[r0 : r1 + 1, c0 : c1 + 1]\n        assert np.all(sub != bg), \"legend marker must be a full 2x2 foreground block\"\n        assert np.all(sub[:, 0] == sub[0, 0]), \"legend source column must be monochrome\"\n        assert np.all(sub[:, 1] == sub[0, 1]), \"legend halo column must be monochrome\"\n        source = int(sub[0, 0])\n        halo = int(sub[0, 1])\n        assert source != halo, \"legend source and halo colors must differ\"\n        assert source not in markers, \"each source color must have exactly one legend marker\"\n\n        markers[source] = halo\n        marker_source_cells[source] = {tuple(map(int, coord)) for coord in comp[\"coords\"] if int(g[tuple(coord)]) == source}\n\n    assert markers, \"expected at least one left-edge legend marker\"\n\n    for source, halo in markers.items():\n        objects = []\n        for comp in find_components(g, bg=bg, connectivity=4, monochromatic=True, colors=[source], return_format=\"dict\"):\n            cells = {tuple(map(int, coord)) for coord in comp[\"coords\"]}\n            if cells <= marker_source_cells[source]:\n                continue\n            objects.append(comp)\n        assert objects, \"each legend source color must label at least one non-marker object\"\n\n        for obj in objects:\n            r0, c0, r1, c1 = obj[\"bbox\"]\n            pr0 = max(0, int(r0) - 1)\n            pc0 = max(0, int(c0) - 1)\n            pr1 = min(h - 1, int(r1) + 1)\n            pc1 = min(w - 1, int(c1) + 1)\n\n            patch = np.full((pr1 - pr0 + 1, pc1 - pc0 + 1), bg, dtype=int)\n            coords = obj[\"coords\"]\n            patch[coords[:, 0] - pr0, coords[:, 1] - pc0] = source\n            enclosed = object_interior(patch, background=bg)\n            fill_mask = (patch == bg) & ~enclosed\n\n            input_region = g[pr0 : pr1 + 1, pc0 : pc1 + 1]\n            target = out[pr0 : pr1 + 1, pc0 : pc1 + 1]\n            assert np.all(input_region[fill_mask] == bg), \"halo cells must be background in the input\"\n            assert np.all((target[fill_mask] == bg) | (target[fill_mask] == halo)), \"halo regions must not conflict\"\n            target[fill_mask] = halo\n\n    return out\n\n# --------------- generator (att1, Claude) ---------------\n\n# Canvas and color palette.\nBACKGROUND = 0\nGRID_SIZE_MIN, GRID_SIZE_MAX = 20, 26\nAVAILABLE_COLORS = tuple(range(1, 10))\n...\ndef generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n    for _ in range(GENERATION_RETRIES):\n        result = _gen_attempt()\n        if result is not None:\n            return result\n    raise RuntimeError(f\"Failed to generate valid input after {GENERATION_RETRIES} attempts\")\n...\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
